Fermix for Linux is one package. `fermix-desktop` carries the window, the
assistant itself, and the toolkit the window draws with, so one install is the
whole product and there is nothing to pair it with.

On a machine with no desktop, install `fermix` instead. It is the same assistant
without the window, for a server, and it is published on the engine's own
release page. The two are alternatives rather than layers: installing either one
replaces the other, and your settings are kept because neither package owns
them.

## Debian, Ubuntu, Mint, Pop!_OS, elementary

```sh
sudo apt install ./fermix-desktop_{{VERSION}}_$(dpkg --print-architecture).deb
```

## Fedora, RHEL, CentOS Stream, Alma, Rocky

```sh
sudo dnf install ./fermix-desktop-{{VERSION}}-1.$(uname -m).rpm
```

## openSUSE and SLE

```sh
sudo zypper install ./fermix-desktop-{{VERSION}}-1.$(uname -m).rpm
```

Leap 15.6 is below the glibc floor named below, so zypper refuses it rather than
installing something that cannot start. Tumbleweed is above the floor.

## Then

Open Fermix from your launcher and it will take you through setup. On a machine
with no desktop, run `fermix setup` in a terminal instead.

## Verifying what you downloaded

Every file on this page is signed with a keyless Sigstore certificate and carries
its own `.sha256`, `.sig` and `.pem` beside it. Name the file you downloaded and
check both:

```sh
package=fermix-desktop_{{VERSION}}_amd64.deb   # or the rpm, or the arm64 deb

sha256sum -c "$package.sha256"

cosign verify-blob \
  --certificate "$package.pem" \
  --signature "$package.sig" \
  --certificate-identity {{DESKTOP_IDENTITY}} \
  --certificate-oidc-issuer https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com \
  "$package"
```

## Requirements

glibc 2.34 or later, which every supported release of Ubuntu, Debian, Fedora and
the RHEL family has carried since 2021. The package declares it, so your package
manager refuses the install on a host below that floor rather than letting it
fail when the window is opened.

The window needs no GTK from you: it carries its own, and everything under it,
so it draws the same on Ubuntu 22.04 as on Fedora 44. What it does use from your
system is the graphics driver, the session bus, the X11 client libraries and
your installed fonts, and your package manager brings any of those you are
missing.

## Where your keys are kept

Keys you give Fermix go to your desktop's own keyring through the freedesktop
Secret Service, so the package needs `secret-tool` and asks for it. It also
*recommends* `gnome-keyring`, which is a suggestion rather than a requirement:
if you already run KWallet, KeePassXC or another Secret Service, refuse the
recommendation and Fermix will use yours.

```sh
sudo apt install --no-install-recommends ./fermix-desktop_{{VERSION}}_$(dpkg --print-architecture).deb
sudo dnf install --setopt=install_weak_deps=False ./fermix-desktop-{{VERSION}}-1.$(uname -m).rpm
```

On a machine with no keyring at all, such as a server or a desktop that runs
none, Fermix says so plainly and offers to keep keys in a file it owns, readable
only by you, inside your Fermix home. That is a choice you make rather than
something it does quietly, and it is reversible later: if you set a keyring up
afterwards, **Use the Keyring Instead** moves every key across for you, so you
never have to find and retype them.

## What it installs, and what removing it leaves

The window and its toolkit live under `/usr/lib/fermix-desktop`. The assistant is
at `/usr/bin/fermix` with its own files under `/usr/lib/fermix`, at exactly the
paths the engine-only package uses.

Removing the package leaves two things on purpose. `/var/lib/fermix/runtimes`
holds the loader a running assistant asks the kernel for every time it starts a
helper, a few hundred kilobytes per version, and deleting it under a running
assistant would break it. Your own configuration, your Fermix home and anything
you have saved are yours, and no package owns them.

Every bundled component, its version and its licence are listed in
`/usr/share/doc/fermix-desktop/copyright`, and
`/usr/share/doc/fermix-desktop/runtime-manifest.json` records the exact source
each one was built from, so you can check any of it from the installed machine.
